courses/coe-318/ch01-software-developmen.content:
Overview



                  Readings Lecture 1 · week 2 · no textbook chapter (Head First Java ch. 1–2 covers the same ground) 



Software is built in a repeating cycle of requirements → design → implementation → testing → deployment → maintenance. This course focuses on the implementation part using object-oriented programming (OOP) in Java: instead of writing one long top-to-bottom program, you define objects that each hold their own data and behaviour, then link them together.
Notes
Programming languages and compiling
A programming language is a high-level language (Java, C, C++, Python) whose instructions control what a computer does. The CPU can’t run it directly, so it has to be compiled (translated) into low-level machine code first.
Software Development Lifecycle (SDLC)
The SDLC is the series of activities used to build software:

































PhaseWhat happensRequirements / analysisFigure out what the software must do, and for whomDesignDecide the structure: which classes, how they interactImplementationWrite the codeTestingCheck the code actually meets the requirementsDeploymentRelease it to usersMaintenanceFix bugs and add features after release
Different SDLC models arrange these phases differently: waterfall (each phase once, in order), iterative, agile, V model, RAD, and spiral.



                  The cost of a design change grows roughly exponentially the later it happens in a project. A change that&#039;s a quick edit during design can mean rewriting and retesting everything after deployment. This is why the design phase matters.



Ethics and software failures
Software increasingly runs things people’s lives depend on and makes decisions about people, so bugs and biases have real consequences. The lecture’s examples:

Medical: an infusion pump (2015) that could fail to delay an infusion or miss doses depending on input timing
Aviation: an F-35 sensor glitch (2016) misreading targets in formation; the Boeing 737 Max (2020) warning light turning on when it shouldn’t
Bias: face recognition trained on non-diverse data mislabelling people (2021); a hiring tool that discriminated against women (2018)




                  Ethics isn&#039;t only a concern for big companies. It&#039;s built into the small decisions every developer makes (privacy, access, fairness), and &quot;the technology did it&quot; is not an excuse. 



Language paradigms























ParadigmIdeaStrengthWeaknessStructuredSteps run top to bottom: get input → process → outputEasy to follow for small programsHard to manage large projects with many developersObject-orientedDefine objects first (a window, a text box, a button, a text processor), then link themObjects can be reused across larger projectsMore up-front design
Many modern languages (e.g. JavaScript) are multi-paradigm and support both.
Objects and classes

An object is a “thing” with a state (its data) and behaviours (what it can do).
A class is the template that defines what kind of object it is: which variables it has and which methods it can run.

One class, many objects: a single Car class can produce a miniVan and a sedan, each with its own speed and licence plate.
Why Java

Simple and object-oriented
Platform independent: source code compiles to bytecode (.class files), which runs on the Java Virtual Machine (JVM) on any OS
Safe: no pointers, and programs live inside the JVM
Multi-threaded
Garbage collected: you never free memory by hand

How Java works:

Write source code in HelloWorld.java
javac compiles it to bytecode, HelloWorld.class
The JVM on the target machine runs the bytecode

We use NetBeans as the IDE (free from netbeans.apache.org). Its autocomplete is worth leaning on while learning.
Java code structure
Code is nested in three levels:

























LevelContainsExampleSource file (.java)One public classHelloWorld.javaClassVariables and methods, between { and }public class HelloWorld { ... }MethodStatements, between { and }public static void main(String[] args) { ... }
Execution always starts at main:
public class HelloWorld {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(&quot;Hello World!&quot;);
    }
}
Variables: declaration and assignment
A variable is a container for a value. Declaring it gives its type (what it can hold) and name; assigning puts a value in.
int age;            // declaration: type then name
float weight;
String name;

age = 20;           // assignment: variable = value;
weight = 145;
name = &quot;Dustin&quot;;
Comments and console output
// single-line comment
/* multi-line
   comment */

System.out.print(&quot;No newline after this&quot;);
System.out.println(&quot;Newline after this&quot;);
print leaves the cursor on the same line; println moves to the next line afterward.
Methods
A method is a unit of work that always runs together. It has a return type, a name, and parameters:
float sum(float a, float b, float c) {   // return type, name, parameters
    float total = a + b + c;
    return total;
}

float result = sum(23.0f, 24.0f, 25.0f);  // calling it
A first class: Car
public class Car {
    private String licensePlate;   // instance variables (the state)
    private double speed;
    private double maxSpeed;

    // constructor: same name as the class, runs when an object is created
    public Car(String plateNumber, double speedVal, double maxVal) {
        licensePlate = plateNumber;
        speed = speedVal;
        maxSpeed = maxVal;
    }

    public void setLicensePlate(String plateNumber) {   // methods (the behaviour)
        licensePlate = plateNumber;
    }
    public void setSpeed(double speedVal) {
        speed = speedVal;
    }
    public double getSpeed() {
        return speed;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Car c = new Car(&quot;C142&quot;, 30, 130);   // new + constructor creates an object
        System.out.println(c.getSpeed());   // . accesses its methods → 30.0
        Car d = new Car(&quot;D234&quot;, 40, 140);
        System.out.println(d.getSpeed());   // → 40.0
    }
}

private / public are access modifiers (covered in Ch 5)
new Car(...) creates an object and runs the constructor
The . operator reaches into a specific object: c.getSpeed() and d.getSpeed() return different values because each object has its own speed

Good practice and naming

Start from a working program, change it, break it, figure out what broke, then fix it
Indent everything between { and }; use blank lines for readability
Use meaningful names: averageGrade, not dfiefjek

CamelCase convention:




















KindRuleExamplesClassStarts with a capital, each word capitalizedHelloWorld, PersonalProfileVariable / methodStarts lowercase, each later word capitalizedfirstName, getName()
Key syntax
Program skeleton
public class ClassName {                      // file must be ClassName.java
    public static void main(String[] args) {  // execution starts here
        // statements;
    }
}
Method
returnType methodName(type param1, type param2) {
    // statements
    return value;   // omit if returnType is void
}

returnType: the type of value sent back, or void for none
methodName: lowercase camelCase
param1, param2: inputs, each with its own type

Creating and using an object
ClassName obj = new ClassName(args);   // runs the matching constructor
obj.method();                           // call a method on that object
Examples
Example 1: print vs println

Lecture 1, console output

What does this print?
System.out.print(&quot;Welcome to COE318!&quot;);
System.out.print(&quot;Hope it will be fun!&quot;);
System.out.println(&quot;Welcome to COE318!&quot;);
System.out.println(&quot;Hope it will be fun!&quot;);
print doesn’t add a newline, so the first two run together on one line. The third println continues on that same line and then ends it:
Welcome to COE318!Hope it will be fun!Welcome to COE318!
Hope it will be fun!

Example 2: String concatenation in output

Lecture 1, StatementExample.java

int age;
float weight;
age = 5;
System.out.println(&quot;My dog is &quot; + age + &quot;years old.&quot;);
weight = 10;
System.out.println(&quot;My dog weighs &quot; + weight + &quot; pounds.&quot;);

+ with a String joins text, so age is converted to &quot;5&quot;. There’s no space before years in the literal, so the output is My dog is 5years old.
weight is a float, so 10 is stored as 10.0 and printed that way: My dog weighs 10.0 pounds.




                  Spaces inside string literals are exactly what gets printed. Java never adds one for you. 



Example 3: Spot the bugs

Lecture 1, method slide

float sum(float numberOne, float numberTwo, float numberThree) {
    float returnVal = numberOne + numberTwo + NumberThree;
    return returnVal;
}
float result = sum(23.0, 24.0, 25.0);

NumberThree ≠ numberThree. Java is case-sensitive, so this is an undefined variable: compile error.
23.0 is a double literal. Passing a double where a float is expected is a narrowing conversion, so it won’t compile. Use 23.0f (or make the parameters double).

Common mistakes

Forgetting Java is case-sensitive: Main, Class, Public are not keywords, and numberThree ≠ NumberThree
Naming the file something other than the public class name + .java
Missing a semicolon at the end of a statement
Unmatched braces { } (indentation makes these easy to spot)
Expecting print to add a newline, or + to add spaces between joined strings

courses/coe-318/ch02-classes-and-objects.content:
Overview



                  Readings Lecture 2 · week 2 · Reference ch. 1–2 



Programming comes down to three things: store information (variables and types), operate on it (operators), and control which operations run and how often (if, switch, loops). Classes then group related data and behaviour together so you can create many independent objects from one template.



                  Trace like the computer. Don&#039;t guess what a program does from its shape: run it in your head one statement at a time and write down every variable&#039;s value as it changes. 



Notes
Primitive data types



























































TypeSizeRange / meaningTypical usebyte1 byte, signed−128 to 127Raw datashort2 bytes, signed−32 768 to 32 767Small integersint4 bytes, signedabout ±2.1 billionDefault whole numberlong8 bytes, signedabout ±9.2 × 10¹⁸Very large whole numbersfloat4 bytes~7 significant digitsDecimals (needs f: 3.5f)double8 bytes~15 significant digitsDefault decimalchar2 bytes, unsigned0 to 65 535 (Unicode)A single character &#039;A&#039;boolean—true / falseConditions
Arithmetic operators
+, -, *, /, and % (remainder: 8 % 3 is 2).



                   Integer division throws away the fraction. If both operands are integers, / gives an integer: 7 / 2 is 3, not 3.5. It truncates, it doesn&#039;t round. Make one operand a double (7.0 / 2) to get 3.5.



% is handy for divisibility checks: n % 2 == 0 means n is even.
The overloaded +

Both operands numbers → addition: 2 + 3 is 5
Either operand a String → concatenation: &quot;Hello&quot; + 2 is &quot;Hello2&quot;

With several operands, + runs left to right, and switches to concatenation the moment it meets a String. Parentheses force an order:

























ExpressionStepsResult&quot;1&quot; + 2 + 3&quot;12&quot; then &quot;123&quot;&quot;123&quot;1 + 2 + &quot;3&quot;3 then &quot;33&quot;&quot;33&quot;&quot;1&quot; + (2 + 3)5 first, then &quot;15&quot;&quot;15&quot;
Comparison operators

























OperatorMeaning==equal to!=not equal to&gt; / &lt;greater / less than&gt;= / &lt;=greater / less than or equal to



                   = assigns, == compares. x = 3 changes x; x == 3 asks a yes/no question and gives a boolean.



Compound assignment and increment



































OperatorExampleMeans+=x += yx = x + y-=x -= yx = x - y*=x *= yx = x * y/=x /= yx = x / y%=x %= yx = x % y
Prefix vs postfix only matters when the increment is part of a bigger expression:

++i: increase i first, then use the new value
i++: use the current value, then increase i

int i = 5;
int a = ++i;   // i becomes 6, a = 6
int b = i++;   // b = 6, then i becomes 7
--i and i-- work the same way.
Conditionals
if (x == 3) {
    System.out.println(&quot;x must be 3&quot;);
} else if (x == 2) {
    System.out.println(&quot;x must be 2&quot;);
} else {
    System.out.println(&quot;Wrong value&quot;);
}
Only the first true branch runs. Anything after the whole if chain runs regardless.
Conditional (ternary) operator: a one-line if/else that produces a value:
String result = grade &gt;= 50 ? &quot;passed&quot; : &quot;failed&quot;;
//              condition   ? if true  : if false
Switch
switch evaluates an expression and jumps to the matching case:
switch (x) {
    case 3:
        System.out.println(&quot;x must be 3&quot;);
        break;
    case 2:
        System.out.println(&quot;x must be 2&quot;);
        break;
    default:
        System.out.println(&quot;Wrong value&quot;);
        break;
}

default runs if no case matches (optional, usually last)
break ends the switch. Without break, execution falls through into the next case’s statements, even though that case didn’t match.

Loops





























LoopChecks conditionRuns at least once?Use whenwhileBefore each passNoUnknown number of repeatsforBefore each passNoCounting a known number of timesdo-whileAfter each passYesBody must run once (e.g. a menu)
while (x &lt; 4) { ...; x = x + 1; }

for (x = 1; x &lt; 4; x++) { ... }    // init; condition; update

do { ... } while (x != 1);         // note the semicolon



                   Infinite loops happen when nothing in the body moves the condition toward false, e.g. forgetting x = x + 1 in a while.



Classes and objects
A class is a template; it can contain only variable declarations and methods. You can’t put loose statements in a class outside a method.
class ClassName {
    type var1;            // fields (instance variables)
    type var2;

    type method1(parameters) {
        // statements
    }
}
An object is one instance of a class, made with new. Its variables are called instance variables because each instance gets its own copy.
Creating an object is two steps (usually written as one line):

Declare a reference variable: Car miniVan; holds null (refers to nothing yet)
Create the object and store a reference to it: miniVan = new Car(); allocates memory for a new Car and points miniVan at it

Car miniVan = new Car();   // both steps at once
Accessing an object uses the dot operator:
miniVan.speed = 100;       // object.variable (works, but bad practice)
miniVan.setSpeed(50);      // object.method()  (preferred)



                  Good practice: don&#039;t access instance variables directly. Give each one you want exposed a getter (getSpeed()) and setter (setSpeed()). Ch 5 makes this enforceable with private.



Where does main go?
Execution starts at main, so something has to create the objects. Two options:

Put main inside the class itself: works, but mixes test code with the class
Better: a separate tester class (CarTest.java) containing only main, in the same project

public class CarTest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Car miniVan = new Car();
        Car sedan = new Car();
        miniVan.setSpeed(100);
        sedan.setSpeed(110);
        System.out.println(miniVan.getSpeed());   // 100.0
        System.out.println(sedan.getSpeed());     // 110.0
    }
}
Each object has its own copy of the instance variables, so setting the sedan’s speed doesn’t touch the minivan’s.
Methods
ReturnType methodName(parameter-list) {
    // body
}

ReturnType: any primitive or object type, or void if nothing is returned
methodName: no spaces, lowercase first word, capitalize the rest
parameter-list: comma-separated type name pairs

Methods act on the object’s data and can call other methods, in the same class or in other classes. Objects interact by calling each other’s methods, which is how OOP models the real world.
Key syntax
Loops
for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) { }      // runs n times (i = 0 … n-1)
while (condition) { }
do { } while (condition);            // runs at least once
Loop iteration counts for for (x = a; x &lt; b; x++):
\text{iterations} = b - a \qquad (\text{use } b - a + 1 \text{ for } x \le b)

a: starting value of the counter
b: the bound in the condition

Nested loops multiply: an outer loop of m passes around an inner loop of n passes runs the inner body m \times n times.
Ternary
result = condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse;
Examples
Example 1: Loop counts

Lecture 2, in-class questions

How many times does each loop run (or what’s x at the end)?













































CodeAnswerWhyint x=0; for (int i=0;i&lt;5;i++) x = x+i;x = 100+1+2+3+4int x=10; while (x&gt;0) { print; x=x-1; }10 timesx = 10, 9, \ldots, 1for (x=1; x&lt;40; x++)39 times40 - 1int x=3; while (x&lt;40) { print; x=x+1; }37 times40 - 3int x=3; while (x&lt;40) { print; }Infinitex never changesint x=50; do { print; } while (x&lt;40);1 timedo-while runs once before checkingfor (x=1;x&lt;40;x++) for (y=1;y&lt;40;y++) print;1521 times39 \times 39
Example 2: The + operator

Lecture 2

What does each line print?
System.out.println(&quot;1&quot; + 2 + 3);
System.out.println(1 + 2 + &quot;3&quot;);
System.out.println(&quot;1&quot; + (2 + 3));

Starts with a String, so everything is concatenation: 123
1 + 2 are both numbers, so that’s 3 first; then 3 + &quot;3&quot; is 33
Parentheses run first: 2 + 3 = 5, then &quot;1&quot; + 5 is 15

Example 3: Switch without break

Like the Lecture 2 switch question

What prints if x = 2?
switch (x) {
    case 1: System.out.println(&quot;one&quot;);
    case 2: System.out.println(&quot;two&quot;);
    case 3: System.out.println(&quot;three&quot;);
            break;
    default: System.out.println(&quot;other&quot;);
}

Jump to case 2: and print two
No break, so fall through into case 3 and print three
break ends the switch, so other never prints

Output: two, then three.
Example 4: Extending the Car class

Lecture 2, step-by-step exercise

Add to Car: (1) a fuel variable, (2) a getter and setter for it, (3) refuel(amount) that adds fuel, (4) drive(speed) that uses 0.5 fuel per unit of speed. Then trace the test.
public class Car {
    String licensePlate;
    double speed;
    double maxSpeed;
    double fuel;                           // (1)

    public double getFuel() { return fuel; }          // (2)
    public void setFuel(double val) { fuel = val; }

    public void refuel(double amount) {    // (3)
        fuel = fuel + amount;
    }
    public void drive(double speed) {      // (4)
        fuel = fuel - speed * 0.5;
    }
    // ...setLicensePlate, setSpeed, getSpeed as before
}
Car miniVan = new Car();
miniVan.setFuel(0);
miniVan.refuel(20);
System.out.println(miniVan.getFuel());
miniVan.refuel(20);
System.out.println(miniVan.getFuel());
miniVan.drive(20);
System.out.println(miniVan.getFuel());






























StatementfuelPrintssetFuel(0)0.0refuel(20)20.020.0refuel(20)40.040.0drive(20)40 - 20(0.5) = 30.030.0



                  Inside drive, the parameter speed hides the instance variable speed, so speed * 0.5 uses the argument (20), not the car&#039;s stored speed. More on this in Ch 5.



Common mistakes

Expecting 7 / 2 to be 3.5 (integer division gives 3)
Using = instead of == in a condition
Forgetting break in a switch case and getting fall-through
Forgetting to update the loop variable, causing an infinite loop
Off-by-one loop counts: i &lt; n runs n times from 0, i &lt;= n runs n + 1
Assuming a do-while skips the body when the condition starts false (it always runs once)
Writing statements directly in a class body instead of inside a method

courses/coe-318/ch03-variables.content:
Overview



                  Readings Lecture 3 · weeks 2–4 · Reference ch. 3 



This chapter is about what Java is actually doing with variables as a program runs: overloading and constructors, the this keyword, the different kinds of variables, casting, where things live in memory (stack vs heap), what happens when references are copied, and arrays.
Notes
Method overloading
Overloading means several methods share a name but have different signatures: a different number of parameters, or different parameter types.
public void accelerate() {              // no parameters
    speed = speed + 10;
}
public void accelerate(double amount) { // one double
    speed = speed + amount;
}
Java picks which one to run from the arguments: accelerate() adds 10, accelerate(25) adds 25. (This isn’t allowed in C.)



                  Changing only the return type is not overloading. int f(int x) and double f(int x) won&#039;t compile together, because a call f(3) can&#039;t tell them apart.



Mutable vs immutable classes

Mutable: the object’s state can change after it’s created (it has setters). Car is mutable, which makes sense since speed and fuel naturally change.
Immutable: state is set once, in the constructor, and can never change. No setters, and the fields are private.

public class Person {           // immutable
    private String name;
    private boolean isMale;

    public Person(String n, boolean x) {   // the only way to set the fields
        name = n;
        isMale = x;
    }
    public String getName() { return name; }
    public boolean isMale() { return isMale; }
}
Immutability protects data that shouldn’t change after creation (a licence plate, a voter registration) from buggy or malicious code.



                  To check if a class is immutable, look for two things: all fields private, and no method that changes a field after construction.



Constructors
A constructor initializes a new object. Its rules:

Same name as the class
No return type, not even void
Runs automatically on new

The default constructor. If you write no constructor, Java generates a no-argument one that does nothing. As soon as you write any constructor, that free one disappears.
public class Car {
    public Car(String plate, double speed, double max) { ... }
}

Car a = new Car(&quot;ABC&quot;, 50, 100);   // OK
Car b = new Car();                 // compile error: no Car() exists anymore
Constructors can be overloaded like methods. Add public Car() { } and both calls work.
The this keyword
this is a reference to the current object, the one the method or constructor was called on. Writing speed inside a method is really shorthand for this.speed.
Its main use is when a parameter has the same name as a field:
public Car(String licensePlate, double speed, double maxSpeed) {
    this.licensePlate = licensePlate;   // field = parameter
    this.speed = speed;
    this.maxSpeed = maxSpeed;
}
Without this., speed = speed; would assign the parameter to itself and leave the field unchanged.
Kinds of variables
By data type:




















KindHoldsExamplesPrimitiveThe value itselfint a, double d, char cReferenceThe address of an objectCar miniVan, String name, int[] scores
By where they’re declared:
public class C {
    private int i;          // instance variable: belongs to the object
    public void f(int j) {  // j: parameter
        int k;              // k: local variable
        k = 2 * j;
        i = i + k;
    }
    public void g() {
        int k;              // a different local k, unrelated to f&#039;s
    }
}
Classes are data types
Once you define a class, it’s a type like any other. You can use it for variables (Car sedan;), return types (public Car giveMeACar()), and parameters:
public boolean isSameSpeed(Car a, Car b) {
    return a.getSpeed() == b.getSpeed();
}
Type conversion and casting
Widening (small → large type) happens automatically and is safe:
\texttt{byte} \to \texttt{short} \to \texttt{int} \to \texttt{long} \to \texttt{float} \to \texttt{double}
Narrowing (large → small) can lose information, so Java makes you ask for it with a cast: put the target type in parentheses.
int total = 40, count = 30;
float result1 = total / count;          // int division FIRST → 1, then widened → 1.0
float result2 = (float) total / count;  // total becomes 40.0f first → 1.3333334
int back = (int) result2;               // narrowing: drops the fraction → 1



                  The cast must happen before the division. (float)(total / count) is still 1.0, because the integer division already threw away the fraction.



Initialization





























VariableDefault value if you don’t set oneLocal variableNone. Using it before assigning is a compile error.Instance variable (number)0 / 0.0Instance variable (char)&#039;\0&#039;Instance variable (boolean)falseInstance variable (reference)null
null is a keyword meaning “refers to no object.”
Memory: stack and heap




















RegionWhat lives thereLifetimeStackLocal variables and parameters (including reference variables themselves)Pushed when a method is called, freed automatically when it returnsHeapEvery object created with new (and its instance variables)Until nothing refers to it, then garbage collected
For Car car1 = new Car(&quot;ABC&quot;, 20, 100); inside main:

car1 is on the stack. It holds only an address (4–8 bytes).
The Car object with &quot;ABC&quot;, 20, 100 is on the heap.

References and garbage collection
Assigning one reference to another copies the address, not the object:
Car miniVan = new Car(&quot;ABC&quot;, 50, 100);
Car sedan = new Car();
sedan = miniVan;     // sedan now points at the SAME object as miniVan
After the last line, both variables refer to the &quot;ABC&quot; car. The no-argument Car that sedan used to point at has no references left, so it’s eligible for garbage collection. Java tracks how many references point at each heap object; at zero, the garbage collector can reclaim the memory. You never free memory yourself.
Arrays
An array holds a fixed number of values of one type (primitive or object), indexed from 0 to N - 1.
int[] scores = new int[10];                     // 10 ints, all 0
int[] units = {147, 323, 89, 933, 540};         // initializer list
char[] grades = {&#039;A&#039;, &#039;B&#039;, &#039;C&#039;, &#039;D&#039;, &#039;F&#039;};

scores[0] = 79;                                 // access by index
System.out.println(units.length);               // 5 (length is a field, no parentheses)

An index outside 0 … length - 1 throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException at run time
An array is an object, so the variable is a reference. alias = scores; makes both names point at the same array.




                  C-style int scores[10]; does not compile in Java. Size goes with new: int[] scores = new int[10];



Arrays of objects hold references, which start as null. Creating the array doesn’t create any objects:
Books[] myBooks = new Books[2];   // two null references, zero Books
myBooks[0] = new Books();         // now one actual Books object
myBooks[0].title = &quot;The Grapes of Java&quot;;
Key syntax
Constructor and this
public ClassName(Type field) {   // no return type
    this.field = field;          // this.field = instance variable
}
Cast
double avg = (double) sum / n;   // cast one operand BEFORE dividing
Arrays
Type[] a = new Type[N];          // indices 0 … N-1
a.length                         // number of elements
for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) { a[i] ... }
Examples
Example 1: Which constructor runs?

Lecture 3, “Find output”

public class Cube {
    int length, breadth, height;

    public int getVolume() { return length * breadth * height; }

    Cube() { length = 10; breadth = 10; height = 10; }
    Cube(int l, int b, int h) { length = l; breadth = b; height = h; }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Cube cubeObj1 = new Cube();
        Cube cubeObj2 = new Cube(10, 20, 30);
        System.out.println(&quot;Volume of Cube1 is: &quot; + cubeObj1.getVolume());
        System.out.println(&quot;Volume of Cube2 is: &quot; + cubeObj2.getVolume());
    }
}

new Cube() has no arguments, so the no-arg constructor runs: 10 \times 10 \times 10 = 1000
new Cube(10, 20, 30) matches the three-int constructor: 10 \times 20 \times 30 = 6000

Volume of Cube1 is: 1000
Volume of Cube2 is: 6000

Example 2: Will it compile?

Lecture 3, constructor questions

public class DefaultConstructorOnly { int size = 5; }

public class OneConstructor {
    OneConstructor(String message) { System.out.println(message); }
}

public class TwoConstructors {
    TwoConstructors(String message) { System.out.println(message); }
    TwoConstructors() { System.out.println(&quot;No argument Constructor&quot;); }
}






























StatementCompiles?Whynew DefaultConstructorOnly()YesNo constructor written, so Java supplies DefaultConstructorOnly()new TwoConstructors()YesA no-arg constructor is explicitly writtennew OneConstructor()NoWriting OneConstructor(String) removed the default onenew Car(&quot;ABC&quot;, 50) (Car has 0-arg and 3-arg)NoNo constructor takes (String, double)
Example 3: Casting and the GPA

Lecture 3, Student exercise

Complete the constructor so it computes GPA = totalGradePoints / totalHours, write getGPA(), and create a student &quot;Sanders, Bruce&quot; with 35 hours and 92 grade points.
class Student {
    private String name;
    private int totalHours;
    private int totalGradePoints;
    private double GPA;

    public Student(String name, int totalHours, int totalGradePoints) {
        this.name = name;
        this.totalHours = totalHours;
        this.totalGradePoints = totalGradePoints;
        GPA = (double) totalGradePoints / totalHours;   // cast!
    }

    public double getGPA() { return GPA; }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student bSanders = new Student(&quot;Sanders, Bruce&quot;, 35, 92);
        System.out.println(bSanders.getGPA());
    }
}

this.name = name etc.: the parameters have the same names as the fields, so this. is required
Without the cast, 92 / 35 is integer division = 2, stored as 2.0
With the cast: 92 / 35 = 2.628571\ldots, so it prints 2.6285714285714286

Example 4: Two names for one array

Lecture 3, arrays in memory

int[] integerArray = new int[5];
for (int i = 0; i &lt; integerArray.length; i++)
    integerArray[i] = 5;
int[] alias = integerArray;
alias[3] = 10;
System.out.println(integerArray[3]);   // (a)
integerArray = new int[3];
System.out.println(integerArray[3]);   // (b)
System.out.println(alias[3]);          // (c)

(a) alias and integerArray point at the same array, so changing alias[3] changes integerArray[3]. Prints 10.
integerArray now points at a new 3-element array. alias still points at the old one.
(b) The new array only has indices 0–2, so this throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException and the program stops.
(c) never runs. If (b) were removed, it would print 10: the old array still exists because alias refers to it.

Example 5: Heap quiz

Lecture 3, “Find output”

class HeapQuiz { int id = 0; }

HeapQuiz[] hq = new HeapQuiz[5];
int x = 0;
while (x &lt; 3) {
    hq[x] = new HeapQuiz();
    hq[x].id = x;
    x = x + 1;
}
hq[3] = hq[1];
hq[4] = hq[1];
hq[3] = hq[2];
for (int j = 0; j &lt; 5; j++)
    System.out.println(&quot;hq[&quot; + j + &quot;]:&quot; + hq[j].id);

The loop creates three objects: call them A (id 0), B (id 1), C (id 2) in hq[0..2]
hq[3] = hq[1] → hq[3] points at B
hq[4] = hq[1] → hq[4] points at B
hq[3] = hq[2] → hq[3] now points at C instead






























Index01234ObjectABCCBid01221
hq[0]:0
hq[1]:1
hq[2]:2
hq[3]:2
hq[4]:1




                  Only three objects ever exist. hq[3] and hq[4] are just extra references to existing ones, and nothing is garbage collected.



Common mistakes

“Overloading” by changing only the return type
Adding a constructor with parameters and then calling new X() without writing a no-arg one
speed = speed; in a constructor: forgot this., so the field never gets set
Casting too late: (double)(a / b) still does integer division
Using a local variable before assigning it (compile error, no default value)
Thinking b = a; copies an object. It copies the reference; both now share one object.
Creating an array of objects and forgetting to new each element, then getting a NullPointerException
Indexing a[a.length]: the last valid index is a.length - 1

courses/coe-318/ch04-using-classes-and-o.content:
Overview



                  Readings Lecture 4 · week 4 · Reference ch. 4, 10 



Real programs have many objects that hold references to each other and pass information around. This chapter covers the built-in String class, how to compare values and objects correctly (== vs equals), how Java passes arguments to methods, and how to link objects together and design a small system from a written description.
Notes
The String class
String is a built-in class, so a String variable is a reference. Strings get one special shortcut: you can create them from a literal without new.
String a = &quot;ABC 41&quot;;              // literal: stored in the String pool
String b = &quot;ABC 41&quot;;              // same literal → reuses the SAME object
String c = new String(&quot;ABC 41&quot;);  // new → always a brand-new object
Strings are immutable. Methods like concat, replace, toUpperCase never change the original; they return a new String. Keep the result or it’s lost:
name.toUpperCase();          // does nothing useful: result thrown away
name = name.toUpperCase();   // keeps it
Common methods (indices start at 0):




























































MethodReturnsExample on &quot;ABC 41&quot;length()Number of characters6charAt(i)Character at index icharAt(2) → &#039;C&#039;concat(s)This string + sconcat(&quot;564&quot;) → &quot;ABC 41564&quot;replace(old, new)Every old char replacedreplace(&#039;4&#039;, &#039;0&#039;) → &quot;ABC 01&quot;substring(i)From i to the endsubstring(1) → &quot;BC 41&quot;substring(i, j)From i up to but not including jsubstring(1, 2) → &quot;B&quot;toUpperCase() / toLowerCase()Case-changed copy&quot;abc&quot; → &quot;ABC&quot;equals(s)true if same characters—equalsIgnoreCase(s)Same, ignoring case—compareTo(s)&lt; 0, 0, or &gt; 0 (alphabetical order)—



                   length() has parentheses for Strings (it&#039;s a method). For arrays it&#039;s length with no parentheses (it&#039;s a field).



An index past the end, like substring(1, 8) on a 6-character string, throws StringIndexOutOfBoundsException.
Comparing values






























TypeHow to compareWhyint, long, char==Exact valuesfloat, doubleMath.abs(a - b) &lt; TOLERANCEDecimals are stored approximately, so 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3 is falsechar==, &lt;, &gt;Compares Unicode values: &#039;0&#039; &lt; &#039;L&#039; is trueString / objects.equals()== compares references, not contents
final double TOLERANCE = 0.00000001;
if (Math.abs(a - b) &lt; TOLERANCE) { /* treat as equal */ }
== vs equals()
For references, == asks “are these the same object?” (same address), not “do they hold the same data?”
String name1 = new String(&quot;Lewis&quot;);
String name2 = new String(&quot;Lewis&quot;);
name1 == name2        // false: two separate objects
name1.equals(name2)   // true: same characters



                  Two identical literals ( &quot;Lewis&quot; == &quot;Lewis&quot;) often happen to be true because of the String pool. Don&#039;t rely on it. Always use .equals() for Strings.



Comparing your own objects
For a class you wrote, equals() doesn’t know what “equal” means yet, so by default it behaves just like ==:
Car miniVan = new Car(&quot;ABC&quot;, 50, 100);
Car sedan = new Car(&quot;ABC&quot;, 50, 100);
sedan == miniVan          // false: different objects
sedan.equals(miniVan)     // STILL false: default equals is ==
To compare contents, compare the fields yourself (or later, write your own equals method):
sedan.licensePlate.equals(miniVan.licensePlate)
    &amp;&amp; sedan.speed == miniVan.speed
    &amp;&amp; sedan.maxSpeed == miniVan.maxSpeed     // true



                  Design lesson: you decide what makes two objects equal. Two cars might be &quot;equal&quot; just because their licence plates match.



Parameter passing
Java always passes a copy of what’s in the variable. What that copy means depends on the type:




















Argument typeWhat’s copiedCan the method change the caller’s data?Primitive (int, double, …)The valueNoObject / arrayThe reference (address)Yes, by changing the object it points to
void changeMe(double d) { d = 345.0; }     // changes only the local copy

double d = 2.0;
changeMe(d);
System.out.println(d);                     // 2.0
void changeParameters(Car c) { c.speed = 30; }   // follows the reference

Car miniVan = new Car(&quot;ABC&quot;, 50, 100);
changeParameters(miniVan);
System.out.println(miniVan.speed);               // 30.0



                  Reassigning the parameter itself ( c = new Car(...)) inside the method doesn&#039;t affect the caller. It only redirects the local copy of the reference. Only changes through the reference (c.speed = ...) are visible outside.



Linking objects
A field can hold a reference to another object, which links them. Any reference can also be null, meaning “linked to nothing”.
class Employee {
    String name;
    Employee supervisor;    // a reference to another Employee

    Employee(String name, Employee supervisor) {
        this.name = name;
        this.supervisor = supervisor;
    }
}

Employee ceo = new Employee(&quot;Stacey&quot;, null);   // no supervisor
Employee emp = new Employee(&quot;Gavin&quot;, ceo);     // linked to Stacey

if (emp.supervisor == null)
    System.out.println(emp.name + &quot; is the CEO!&quot;);
else
    System.out.println(&quot;The supervisor of &quot; + emp.name + &quot; is &quot; + emp.supervisor.name);
// The supervisor of Gavin is Stacey
emp.supervisor.name follows two links: from emp to Stacey’s object, then to her name.
Designing from a description
To turn a written problem into classes:

Nouns become classes (Book, Member, Library)
Properties become fields (title, author, ISBN)
“Has many” becomes an array or list field (a Member has borrowed books)
Verbs become methods (borrow, add, register)
Write a tester class that acts out the scenario

Key syntax
Comparisons
s1.equals(s2)                       // String contents
Math.abs(a - b) &lt; TOLERANCE         // floating-point
obj1 == obj2                        // same object?
Substring range
\texttt{s.substring(i, j)} \text{ has length } j - i

i: first index included
j: first index excluded

Examples
Example 1: String methods

Lecture 4, String slides

Given String licensePlate = new String(&quot;ABC 41&quot;);, what is each value?
char a = licensePlate.charAt(2);
String extendedPlate = licensePlate.concat(&quot;564&quot;);
int b = licensePlate.length();
String newPlate = extendedPlate.replace(&#039;4&#039;, &#039;0&#039;);
String sub1 = licensePlate.substring(1);
String sub2 = licensePlate.substring(1, 2);
String sub3 = licensePlate.substring(1, 8);
Indices: A=0, B=1, C=2, space=3, 4=4, 1=5.

a = &#039;C&#039;
extendedPlate = &quot;ABC 41564&quot; (licensePlate itself is unchanged)
b = 6 (the space counts)
newPlate = &quot;ABC 01560&quot;: every &#039;4&#039; in &quot;ABC 41564&quot; is replaced
sub1 = &quot;BC 41&quot;
sub2 = &quot;B&quot; (index 1 only; length 2 - 1 = 1)
sub3 throws StringIndexOutOfBoundsException: index 8 is past the end of a 6-character string

Example 2: Pass by value vs reference

Lecture 4, parameter passing

What does this print?
public static void main(String[] args) {
    int n = 5;
    Car car = new Car(&quot;XYZ&quot;, 50, 100);
    update(n, car);
    System.out.println(n + &quot; &quot; + car.speed);
}
static void update(int n, Car c) {
    n = 99;
    c.speed = 80;
    c = new Car(&quot;NEW&quot;, 0, 0);
    c.speed = 10;
}

n = 99 changes the method’s own copy only. Caller’s n stays 5.
c.speed = 80 follows the reference to the caller’s car. Its speed is now 80.
c = new Car(...) points the local c at a new object. The caller’s car is untouched.
c.speed = 10 changes the new object, which is garbage once the method returns.

Prints 5 80.0.
Example 3: Tracing an array of linked objects

Lecture 4, challenging quiz (Act 1)

class Coord {
    int x, y;
    public Coord(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }
}

Coord[] original, alias;
original = new Coord[6];
int i = 0;
while (i &lt; original.length) {
    original[i] = new Coord(i, original.length - i);
    i++;
}
i = 0;
while (i &lt; original.length) {
    original[i] = original[i + 1];
    i += 2;
}
alias = original;
alias[3].y = alias[0].x;
System.out.println(original[0].x + &quot;,&quot; + original[0].y);
System.out.println(alias[3].x + &quot;,&quot; + alias[3].y);


First loop creates six objects, (i,\ 6 - i):























Index012345Object(0,6)(1,5)(2,4)(3,3)(4,2)(5,1)


Second loop runs for i = 0, 2, 4, copying each odd slot’s reference into the even slot before it:























Index012345Object(1,5)(1,5)(3,3)(3,3)(5,1)(5,1)
Slots 0/1, 2/3 and 4/5 now share objects. The original (0,6), (2,4), (4,2) objects are garbage.


alias = original → same array, two names


alias[3].y = alias[0].x → the (3,3) object’s y becomes 1, so it’s now (3,1). original[2] sees this too.


1,5
3,1

Example 4: Design an Airport class from its output

Lecture 4, design question

Write the Airport class so this main gives the output below.
Airport quizairport = new Airport(5);          // 5 gates
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++)
    quizairport.addPassengers(i, (20 * i + 3)); // (gate index, passengers)
quizairport.printPassengers();
Number of passengers at Gate 1 is 3
Number of passengers at Gate 2 is 23
...
Number of passengers at Gate 5 is 83


Fields: one passenger count per gate → an int array, sized by the constructor
addPassengers(gate, n): store n at index gate
printPassengers(): loop over the array. Gates are indexed from 0 but printed from 1, so print i + 1.

public class Airport {
    private int[] passengers;

    public Airport(int numGates) {
        passengers = new int[numGates];
    }

    public void addPassengers(int gate, int count) {
        passengers[gate] = passengers[gate] + count;
    }

    public void printPassengers() {
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; passengers.length; i++)
            System.out.println(&quot;Number of passengers at Gate &quot; + (i + 1)
                               + &quot; is &quot; + passengers[i]);
    }
}
Example 5: Library system design

Lecture 4, design case study

A Book has a title, author and ISBN. A Member has a name and can borrow multiple books. A Library manages its books and members. John borrows Anne of Green Gables.

Classes from nouns: Book, Member, Library
Fields: Book → title, author, isbn. Member → name, plus an array of borrowed Books. Library → arrays of Books and Members.
Methods from verbs: Member.borrow(Book), Library.addBook(Book), Library.addMember(Member)

public class Book {
    private String title, author, isbn;
    public Book(String title, String author, String isbn) {
        this.title = title; this.author = author; this.isbn = isbn;
    }
    public String getTitle() { return title; }
}

public class Member {
    private String name;
    private Book[] borrowed = new Book[10];
    private int count = 0;

    public Member(String name) { this.name = name; }

    public void borrow(Book b) {
        borrowed[count] = b;   // stores a REFERENCE: no copy of the book is made
        count++;
    }
    public String getName() { return name; }
}

public class Library {
    private Book[] books = new Book[100];
    private Member[] members = new Member[100];
    private int numBooks = 0, numMembers = 0;

    public void addBook(Book b) { books[numBooks++] = b; }
    public void addMember(Member m) { members[numMembers++] = m; }
}

public class LibraryTest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Library lib = new Library();
        Book anne = new Book(&quot;Anne of Green Gables&quot;, &quot;L. M. Montgomery&quot;, &quot;978-0&quot;);
        Member john = new Member(&quot;John&quot;);
        lib.addBook(anne);
        lib.addMember(john);
        john.borrow(anne);
    }
}



                  After john.borrow(anne), the library&#039;s array and John&#039;s array both refer to the same Book object. That&#039;s what linking means: there&#039;s only ever one copy of the book.



Common mistakes

Comparing Strings with == instead of .equals()
Calling s.toUpperCase(); and expecting s to change (Strings are immutable)
substring(i, j) includes i but excludes j
Comparing doubles with ==
Expecting a method to change a primitive argument, or expecting param = new X() to change the caller’s variable
Following a link (emp.supervisor.name) when the link is null, causing a NullPointerException
Writing &quot;... &quot; + emp.name &quot; is ...&quot;: a missing + between pieces won’t compile

courses/coe-318/ch05-writing-classes.content:
Overview



                  Readings Lectures 4–5 · week 5 · Reference ch. 5 



Writing a good class is about controlled interaction. Packages organize where classes live, encapsulation (with private and getters/setters) controls who can touch an object’s data, scope controls where a variable is visible, and Scanner lets the program take input from the user. This chapter also covers tracing chains of objects and passing arrays to methods.
Notes
Packages
A package groups related classes, like a folder. Packages organize code, make classes easy to find, and avoid name clashes (two classes can share a name if they’re in different packages).

















KindExamplesBuilt-injava.lang (String, Math; imported automatically), java.util (Scanner, ArrayList)User-definedcoe318 for your labs
Each package is a directory: classes in package mypack must be in a folder called mypack.
package coe318;            // first line of the file (NetBeans adds it)
public class Lab3 { int labNum = 3; }
To use a class from another package, import it:
import coe318.Lab3;        // one class (preferred)
import coe318.*;           // every class in the package
Encapsulation
Encapsulation means hiding an object’s variables inside the class and only letting other code reach them through methods. This stops main or another object from “reaching in” and setting nonsense values (a negative speed, a changed licence plate).
Visibility modifiers

























ModifierAccessible frompublicAnywhereprivateOnly inside the same class(none): default / packageAny class in the same packageprotectedSame package, plus subclasses (see Ch 9)
class Modifiers {
    public int pubData;
    private int privData;
    private void privMethod() { }
    public void pubMethod() {
        privMethod();          // OK: same class
    }
}

Modifiers mod = new Modifiers();   // in another class:
mod.pubData = 1;       // OK
mod.pubMethod();       // OK
mod.privData = 1;      // compile error
mod.privMethod();      // compile error



                  If main is inside the same class, access modifiers don&#039;t restrict it at all. private only blocks other classes.



Rule of thumb

























MemberVisibilityNameInstance variablesprivate—Methods other classes should callpublicService methodsHelper methods used only inside the classprivateSupport methods
Getters and setters
With private fields, the class provides:

Accessor (getter): returns a field’s value, e.g. getSpeed()
Mutator (setter): changes a field, e.g. setSpeed(double)

Only write getters and setters for fields that should be exposed. Everything else gets set once through the constructor:
public class Car {
    private String licensePlate;   // set once, never exposed for change
    private double speed;          // has getter and setter
    private double maxSpeed;       // set once

    public Car(String plateNumber, double speedVal, double maxVal) {
        licensePlate = plateNumber;
        speed = speedVal;
        maxSpeed = maxVal;
    }
    public void setSpeed(double speedVal) { speed = speedVal; }
    public double getSpeed() { return speed; }
}



                  A setter is where you can validate: if (speedVal &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; speedVal &lt;= maxSpeed) speed = speedVal;. A public field can&#039;t protect itself like this.



Scope




















VariableScopeVisible…Instance variableClass scopeAnywhere in the class, from its { to its }. Effectively “global” within the class.Local variable / parameterBlock scopeOnly inside the { } block where it’s declared
Shadowing: if a local variable or parameter has the same name as an instance variable, the local one hides the field inside that method. Use this.name to reach the field.
public Point(double x, double y) {
    this.x = x;    // field = parameter
    this.y = y;
}
Arrays as parameters




















PassingBehaves likeMethod can change caller’s array?The whole array, f(xyz)An object (reference copied)YesOne element, f(xyz[3])A primitive (value copied)No
2D arrays
A 2D array is an array of arrays (rows of columns):
int[][] A = { {  1,  0, 12, -1 },
              {  7, -3,  2,  5 },
              { -5, -2,  2,  9 } };
int[][] B = new int[3][4];      // 3 rows, 4 columns, all 0

A.length        // 3: number of rows
A[0].length     // 4: columns in row 0
A[1][2]         // 2: row 1, column 2
Traverse with nested loops, rows outside, columns inside:
for (int row = 0; row &lt; A.length; row++)
    for (int col = 0; col &lt; A[row].length; col++)
        System.out.println(A[row][col]);
User input with Scanner
Scanner (in java.util) reads input from the console or a file:
import java.util.Scanner;

Scanner scan = new Scanner(System.in);                     // console
Scanner fileScan = new Scanner(new FileReader(&quot;myfile&quot;));  // file

























MethodReadsnextLine()The whole rest of the line, spaces includednext()One token (up to the next whitespace)nextInt() / nextDouble()One token, converted to a numberhasNextInt() / hasNextDouble()true if the next token is that type (doesn’t consume it)
The program waits at each next...() call until the user types something.



                   nextInt() throws an exception (InputMismatchException) if the user types something that isn&#039;t an int. Check with hasNextInt() first.



Key syntax
Encapsulated class template
public class Name {
    private Type field;                          // private data

    public Name(Type field) { this.field = field; }

    public Type getField() { return field; }     // getter
    public void setField(Type field) {           // setter (only if needed)
        this.field = field;
    }
}
Reading input
Scanner scan = new Scanner(System.in);
String word = scan.next();
int n = scan.nextInt();
double d = scan.nextDouble();
String line = scan.nextLine();
2D array size
\text{elements} = \texttt{rows} \times \texttt{cols} = \texttt{A.length} \times \texttt{A[0].length}
Examples
Example 1: Default vs private access

Lecture 4–5, visibility slides

CarTest is a different class from Car, in the same package. Which lines compile?
// Version 1                          // Version 2
public class Car {                    public class Car {
    String licensePlate;                  private String licensePlate;
    double speed;                         public double speed;
    ...                                   ...
}                                     }

// in CarTest.main:
Car miniVan = new Car(&quot;ABC&quot;, 50, 100);
miniVan.licensePlate = &quot;GHI&quot;;
miniVan.speed = 100;




















LineVersion 1 (default)Version 2miniVan.licensePlate = &quot;GHI&quot;;OK (same package)Error (private)miniVan.speed = 100;OKOK (public)



                  The slide writes miniVan.speed = &quot;100&quot;; (a String into a double). That&#039;s a type error regardless of access.



Example 2: Shadowing and this

Lecture 4–5, Point code sample

What prints for each object?
public class Point {
    private double x, y;

    public Point() { x = 1; y = 1; }

    public Point(double x, double y) {
        this.x = x;
        this.y = y;
        this.x++;
        System.out.println(&quot;this.x = &quot; + this.x);
        System.out.println(&quot;x = &quot; + x);
    }

    public Point(double x, double y, boolean dummy) {
        x++;
        System.out.println(&quot;x = &quot; + x);
    }
}

Point p1 = new Point();
Point p2 = new Point(2, 3);
Point p3 = new Point(2, 3, false);

p1: no parameters, so x and y mean the fields. Sets them to 1.0. Prints nothing.
p2: field x = 2, then this.x++ makes the field 3. The parameter x is still 2.
this.x = 3.0
x = 2.0


p3: no this., so x++ increments the parameter to 3. The fields are never set and stay 0.0.
x = 3.0



Example 3: Recursive object chain

Lecture 4–5, “Find output” (Act 2)

public class A {
    private A next;
    private int v;

    public A(A n, int i) { v = i; next = n; }

    public int getT() {
        int t = v;
        if (next != null)
            t = t + next.getT();
        return t;
    }
}

A a = new A(null, 3);
A b = new A(a, 1);
A c = new A(b, 2);
System.out.println(a.getT());
System.out.println(b.getT());
System.out.println(c.getT());
The links form a chain c \to b \to a \to \texttt{null}. getT() adds this object’s v to everything further down the chain:

a.getT() = 3 (no next)
b.getT() = 1 + 3 = 4
c.getT() = 2 + 4 = 6

Prints 3, 4, 6.
Example 4: Fill in the blanks for the chain

Lecture 4–5, in-class activity

Using the same class A, fill in the blanks so the output is 5, 8, 6.
A a = new A(____, ____);
A ___ = new A(____, 1);
A ___ = new A(____, 2);
System.out.println(a.getT());   // 5
System.out.println(b.getT());   // 8
System.out.println(c.getT());   // 6

a is declared first, so it can’t link to anything yet: next = null. For a.getT() = 5, v = 5.
The second object has v = 1. It can link to null or a. Linking to a gives 1 + 5 = 6, which matches c. So the second variable is c.
The third has v = 2. Linking to c gives 2 + 6 = 8, which matches b.

A a = new A(null, 5);
A c = new A(a, 1);
A b = new A(c, 2);



                  Variables don&#039;t have to be declared in alphabetical order. Work backwards from the totals. 



Example 5: Arrays and elements as arguments

Lecture 4–5, arrays as parameters

What does each aProc print?
void aProc() {                     void aProc() {
    int[] xyz = new int[12];           int[] xyz = new int[12];
    theProc(xyz);                      theProc(xyz[3]);
    System.out.println(xyz[3]);        System.out.println(xyz[3]);
}                                  }
void theProc(int[] mno) {          void theProc(int mno) {
